sections.text:
Pick the path that fits you:

- **Let your coding agent do it.** On [hivemindos.app/superagent-api](https://hivemindos.app/superagent-api/#setup), press **Copy** under "Set it up with your agent" and send it to the agent working on your project. You open one link to sign in, add credits and approve. The agent saves the key in your project's env file and checks it works. See [agent setup](agent-setup.html).
- **Make one yourself** in the [console](https://hivemindos.app/superagent-api/console/#keys).
- **Create one in code** with an existing HivemindOS credit token (shown below).
- **Skip the account.** An agent with an x402 wallet can [pay for a single call](pay-per-call.html) and is charged only if the call succeeds.
- **Earn while you build.** Your first x402 payment is matched, your app earns 5% of what its users spend, and your agent NFTs can earn a fee you set on each reply people get from them (free to chat unless you set one). See [rewards and the Hall](rewards.html).

Keys begin with `hmos_live_`. Keep them on your server: never put one in a browser bundle, mobile app, public repository, log, or analytics event.

To create a key in code, install the SDK:

```bash
npm install https://github.com/LiamVisionary/hivemindos-sdk/releases/download/v1.7.0/hivemindos-sdk-1.7.0.tgz
```

This installs the built SDK from its GitHub release. It works with npm, pnpm and yarn, and is imported as `@hivemindos/sdk`. The API also works with any HTTPS client.

This key can list services and run research, and nothing else:

```ts
import { createHivemindOSApiKey } from "@hivemindos/sdk";

const result = await createHivemindOSApiKey({
 creditToken: process.env.HIVEMINDOS_CREDIT_TOKEN!,
 idempotencyKey: "my-backend-key-v1",
 label: "My backend",
 scopes: ["services:read", "services:invoke"],
 allowedServices: ["hive-research"],
 allowedOperations: [
 "services.list",
 "capabilities.list",
 "actions.list",
 "services.invoke.hive-research.analyses.create",
 ],
 limits: {
 "services.invoke.hive-research.analyses.create": {
 requestsPerMinute: 60,
 maxConcurrent: 2,
 },
 },
});

if (!result.ok) throw new Error(result.error);

// The full secret is returned only now. Save it in your secret manager.
const apiKey = result.secret;
```

`allowedServices` and `allowedOperations` limit what the key can reach. See [authentication and API keys](authentication.html) for every option.

```ts
import { HivemindOSClient } from "@hivemindos/sdk";

const hive = new HivemindOSClient({
 apiKey: process.env.HIVEMINDOS_API_KEY!,
 projectId: process.env.HIVEMINDOS_PROJECT_ID, // optional
});

const catalog = await hive.services.list();
if (!catalog.ok) throw new Error(catalog.error);

const research = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-research",
 "analyses.create",
 { question: "Summarize the strongest evidence for this thesis." },
 { idempotencyKey: "research-thesis-001" },
);

if (!research.ok) throw new Error(research.error);
```

Over plain HTTPS, send the key as a bearer token:

```http
Authorization: Bearer hmos_live_...
```

Every mutation and spending action also needs a unique `Idempotency-Key` header. A retry with the same key and input returns the first result instead of repeating the work.

```ts
const result = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "answer-engine",
 "searches.create",
 {
 query: "How well do heat pumps work below freezing?",
 optimizationMode: "balanced",
 sources: ["web"],
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "heat-pump-question-001" },
);

console.log(result.result.message); // the answer, with [1] style citations
console.log(result.result.sources); // what each citation number points to
```

Each source carries the page title and address, so you can render citations as links:

```json
{
 "message": "Modern cold-climate units keep working near -25 °C [1], though their efficiency falls as it gets colder [2].",
 "sources": [
 { "content": "…", "metadata": { "title": "Cold climate heat pumps", "url": "https://example.com/a", "source": "web", "read": true } },
 { "content": "…", "metadata": { "title": "Efficiency in winter", "url": "https://example.org/b", "source": "web", "read": false } }
 ]
}
```

A source with `read: true` was opened and read in full; the rest are search results with their summary text.

A key needs `services:read` and `services:invoke`, with `answer-engine` in its allowed services. Restrict it to `answer-engine.searches.create` if that is all it should do.

Send the text with the request and add `documents` to `sources`. Nothing is stored: the text is used for this one search and then discarded.

```ts
const result = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "answer-engine",
 "searches.create",
 {
 query: "What COP did unit 3 measure at -19 °C?",
 sources: ["documents"],
 documents: [{ title: "Site log", text: "At -19C the measured COP was 1.9…" }],
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "site-log-question-001" },
);
```

Ask for `["documents", "web"]` and both feed one answer, with citations telling them apart.

A key with `api_keys:manage` can create a child key with `POST /api-keys`, authenticated with the parent's bearer key:

```ts
const workerKey = await hive.apiKeys.create(
 {
 label: "Research worker",
 scopes: ["services:read", "services:invoke", "runs:read", "runs:write"],
 allowedServices: ["hive-research"],
 allowedOperations: [
 "capabilities.list",
 "services.invoke.hive-research.analyses.create",
 "runs.create.hive-research.analyses.create",
 "runs.read",
 ],
 expiresAt: "2026-12-01T00:00:00.000Z",
 limits: {
 "services.invoke.hive-research": {
 requestsPerMinute: 20,
 maxConcurrent: 2,
 },
 },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "research-worker-2026-12" },
);
```

A child key can only be narrower than every key above it:

- Its scopes, services, and operations must all be covered by its ancestors.
- A project-bound key cannot change projects or create an account-level child.
- Its expiry cannot be later than the earliest ancestor expiry.
- It keeps its ancestors' privacy tier and cannot loosen it. See [privacy tiers](privacy.html).
- It keeps its ancestors' end user, if one is bound, and cannot name another. See [act for one of your app's users](projects-security.html#act-for-one-of-your-apps-users).
- Its limits cannot exceed an ancestor's covering limit, and every ancestor limit still applies.
- Delegation is limited to eight levels.

Revoking or expiring a key invalidates all its descendants. A key with `api_keys:manage` can list and revoke only itself and its descendants, never a sibling branch.

```ts
const result = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-research",
 "analyses.create",
 { question: "Map the evidence for and against this decision." },
 { idempotencyKey: "analysis-decision-001" },
);
```

Pass path values separately:

```ts
const result = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "managed-agents",
 "agents.chat",
 { message: "Prepare today's operating brief." },
 {
 pathParameters: { agentId: "agent_..." },
 idempotencyKey: "agent-brief-2026-08-25",
 },
);
```

Capabilities marked `approval: "always"` need a matching approval. The approval is bound to the service, operation, path parameters, query, input, managed files, and connection id, so approving one action cannot authorize changed input or a different credential.

```ts
const requested = await hive.approvals.createServiceAction(
 {
 serviceId: "app-hosting",
 operationId: "sites.publish",
 input: { siteId: "site_...", versionId: "version_..." },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "approval-publish-version-17" },
);

if (!requested.ok) throw new Error(requested.error);

await reviewer.approvals.decide(
 requested.approval.id,
 "approve",
 { idempotencyKey: "decision-publish-version-17" },
);

const published = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "app-hosting",
 "sites.publish",
 { siteId: "site_...", versionId: "version_..." },
 {
 approvalId: requested.approval.id,
 idempotencyKey: "publish-version-17",
 },
);
```

An approval expires, can be rejected, and is used up by one successful matching action. Capabilities marked `policy` follow the service's account policy and may still ask for approval or confirmation.

Search before running GPU work. The server resolves the privacy requirement, filters out ineligible hosts, works out each offer's maximum charge, and recommends an offer with a `listingId` you can pin on the execution call.

```ts
const capacity = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-compute",
 "marketplace.search",
 undefined,
 {
 query: {
 modality: "chat",
 model: "hive-compute/auto",
 privacy: "auto",
 dataSensitivity: "public",
 estimatedInputTokens: 2_000,
 estimatedOutputTokens: 1_000,
 },
 idempotencyKey: "compute-search-public-chat-001",
 },
);

if (!capacity.ok || !capacity.result.available) {
 throw new Error("No eligible compute offer is live.");
}

console.log(capacity.result.privacy.resolved);
console.log(capacity.result.recommended.trust.label);
console.log(capacity.result.recommended.estimatedChargeCredits);
```

| `privacy` | Routing |
|---|---|
| `standard` | Includes compatible community hardware. The host may see inputs and outputs. |
| `confidential` | Requires fresh server-verified hardware attestation and renter-only output encryption. Never downgrades. |
| `auto` | Uses `dataSensitivity`: only `public` and `low` may resolve to Standard. Anything else resolves to Confidential. |

Through the [private or confidential base URL](privacy.html), Hive Compute always resolves to Confidential.

Attested hosts carry a `Confidential Verified` trust badge even on a Standard request. The signed completion receipt records both the request's privacy tier and the worker's trust tier.

Never send wallet secrets, passwords, API keys, private keys, or recovery phrases to a GPU worker in either tier.

Read the live model catalog first:

```ts
const models = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-compute",
 "models.list",
 undefined,
 { idempotencyKey: "compute-models-2026-08-25" },
);
```

For public or low-sensitivity work, choose Standard and pin the recommended offer:

```ts
const completion = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-compute",
 "chat.create",
 {
 model: "hive-compute/auto",
 messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Summarize this public benchmark." }],
 stream: false,
 hivemindos: {
 privacy: "standard",
 dataSensitivity: "public",
 listingId: capacity.result.recommended.listingId,
 },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "risk-summary-001" },
);

if (!completion.ok) throw new Error(completion.error);
console.log(completion.chargedCredits);
console.log(completion.result);
```

For private work, keep an RSA-OAEP private key in your app and send only its SPKI PEM public key. A confidential request without this key, or without eligible attested capacity, fails without charge and never falls back to Standard:

```ts
const privateCompletion = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-compute",
 "chat.create",
 {
 model: "hive-compute/auto",
 messages: [{ role: "user", content: privatePrompt }],
 stream: false,
 hivemindos: {
 privacy: "confidential",
 dataSensitivity: "confidential",
 outputPublicKey: process.env.COMPUTE_OUTPUT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM!,
 },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "private-summary-001" },
);
```

`images.create` generates images, supports the same encrypted-output option, and returns base64 image data in `result`:

```ts
const image = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-compute",
 "images.create",
 {
 model: "hive-compute/image",
 prompt: "A clean studio product photograph",
 n: 1,
 response_format: "b64_json",
 hivemindos: { privacy: "standard", dataSensitivity: "public" },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "product-image-001" },
);
```

Streaming is not available here, because the service reports one exact charge after completion. Use `stream: false` and read `chargedCredits`.

Call `capabilities.list` first to read the live workload kinds, MIME types, limits, billing units, and eligible models. Then:

1. Call `jobs.create` with the workload descriptor and your output public key. Plaintext workload parameters are rejected.
2. Encrypt the input for the returned job and enclave key.
3. Upload the ciphertext as a [managed file](files-connections.html).
4. Attach that file with `artifacts.upload`.
5. Call `jobs.submit` with the encrypted payload and the same output public key.
6. Poll `jobs.get` until the job completes.
7. Download each encrypted result with `artifacts.get`, decrypt it locally, then call `artifacts.acknowledge` to remove it.

Create the draft:

```ts
type ComputeJobDraft = {
 id: string;
 worker: { encryptionKeySha256: string };
};

const created = await hive.services.invokeOperation (
 "hive-compute",
 "jobs.create",
 {
 protocol: "hive-compute.workload.v1",
 kind: "video",
 task: "text-to-video",
 model: selectedModel,
 inputMimeTypes: ["application/octet-stream"],
 outputMimeTypes: ["video/mp4"],
 billingUnit: "second",
 requestedUnits: 1,
 outputPublicKey: process.env.COMPUTE_OUTPUT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM!,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "confidential-video-001" },
);

if (!created.ok) throw new Error(created.error);
```

Upload only ciphertext. `artifacts.upload` declares `requestFormat: "binary"`, takes exactly one managed file, and derives the ciphertext digest from that file's verified record:

```ts
const ciphertextFile = await hive.files.upload(
 {
 name: "job-input.enc",
 contentType: "application/octet-stream",
 bytes: ciphertextBytes,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "confidential-video-input-001" },
);

if (!ciphertextFile.ok) throw new Error(ciphertextFile.error);

await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "hive-compute",
 "artifacts.upload",
 {
 encryptedMimeType: "application/octet-stream",
 encryptionPublicKeySha256: created.result.worker.encryptionKeySha256,
 encryptedKey: wrappedContentKeyBase64,
 chunkSize: ciphertextBytes.byteLength,
 chunks: 1,
 },
 {
 pathParameters: {
 jobId: created.result.id,
 artifactId: `${created.result.id}.input.1`,
 },
 fileIds: [ciphertextFile.file.id],
 idempotencyKey: "confidential-video-artifact-001",
 },
);
```

`artifacts.get` returns encrypted bytes as a JSON envelope with `contentType`, `encoding: "base64"`, and `data`. Input files and returned artifacts are each limited to 25 MB per call.

Testnet assets have no monetary value. Read `assets.list` right before a claim to get the supported network and asset pairs, amount, quota, and exact `priceCredits`:

```ts
const catalog = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "testnet-faucet",
 "assets.list",
 undefined,
 { idempotencyKey: "faucet-assets-2026-08-25" },
);
```

Create a claim with a pair from that list. The `idempotencyKey` inside the input identifies the faucet claim; the one in the options protects the outer invocation:

```ts
type FaucetClaimResult = {
 claim: { id: string; status: string };
};

const claim = await hive.services.invokeOperation (
 "testnet-faucet",
 "claims.create",
 {
 network: selected.network,
 asset: selected.asset,
 recipient: testnetRecipient,
 idempotencyKey: "onboarding-wallet-001",
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "platform-onboarding-wallet-001" },
);

if (!claim.ok) throw new Error(claim.error);
console.log(claim.chargedCredits, claim.result.claim);
```

Retry an uncertain request with both original idempotency keys. Read the claim's state with `claims.get`:

```ts
const status = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "testnet-faucet",
 "claims.get",
 undefined,
 {
 pathParameters: { claimId: claim.result.claim.id },
 idempotencyKey: "claim-status-onboarding-wallet-001",
 },
);
```

Claims belong to the account behind the API key; another account cannot read one by guessing its id. Provider failures release the reservation, and a delivered claim returns the exact final charge.

```ts
const account = await hive.databases.account();
if (!account.ok) throw new Error(account.error);

console.log(account.active, account.tier, account.limits, account.usage);
```

This reports whether database access is active, the plan tier, current limits, this month's usage, and portability support. The returned limits are authoritative for that account.

```ts
import { HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS } from "@hivemindos/sdk";

const provisioned = await hive.databases.provision(
 { confirmation: HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS.provision },
 { idempotencyKey: "customer-database-provision-v1" },
);
```

If the account is not eligible or has reached a plan boundary, show the returned next action to the account owner. There is no plan-name or quota override.

Changes use `POST /databases/actions` with `databases:write`, an idempotency key, and the exact SDK confirmation for the action.

```ts
const database = await hive.databases.mutate(
 {
 action: "create-database",
 workspaceId: 7,
 name: "Customers",
 confirmation: HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS.createDatabase,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "customers-database-v1" },
);

const record = await hive.databases.mutate(
 {
 action: "create-record",
 tableId: 42,
 fields: {
 company: "Example Co",
 active: true,
 score: 91,
 },
 confirmation: HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS.createRecord,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "customer-record-example-co-v1" },
);
```

Put a unique business id in the idempotency key so a retried write returns the original result instead of a duplicate.

| Action | Required fields |
|---|---|
| `create-database` | `workspaceId`, `name`, `confirmation` |
| `create-table` | `databaseId`, `name`, `confirmation` |
| `create-field` | `tableId`, `name`, `fieldType`, `confirmation` |
| `delete-database` | `databaseId`, `confirmation` |
| `delete-table` | `tableId`, `confirmation` |
| `delete-field` | `fieldId`, `confirmation` |
| `create-record` | `tableId`, `fields`, `confirmation` |
| `update-record` | `tableId`, `recordId`, `fields`, `confirmation` |
| `delete-record` | `tableId`, `recordId`, `confirmation` |

Field types: `text`, `long_text`, `number`, `boolean`, `date`, `url`, `email`, `phone_number`, and `file`.

Deleting the workspace permanently removes its databases, records, usage history, and unfinished transfer files. Export anything you need first.

```ts
const deleted = await hive.databases.deprovision(
 { confirmation: HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS.deprovision },
 { idempotencyKey: "customer-database-delete-v1" },
);
```

Repeating the same confirmed request is safe and returns `active: false`.

Upload a ZIP archive of up to 100 MB. The source workspace is kept.

1. Calculate the archive's byte length and lowercase SHA-256 hash.
2. Call `beginUpload` with the file details and exact confirmation.
3. Split the archive by the returned `partSize` and upload numbered parts starting at 1. Each part must be between 1 byte and 8 MB.
4. Call `completeUpload`.
5. Poll `getTransfer` until the copy is `complete` or `failed`.

```ts
const started = await hive.databases.beginUpload(
 {
 fileName: "customer-workspace.zip",
 expectedBytes: archive.byteLength,
 sha256: archiveSha256,
 confirmation: HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS.migrateToCloud,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "workspace-upload-2026-08-24" },
);

if (!started.ok) throw new Error(started.error);

const { migration, partSize } = started;
let partNumber = 1;

for (let offset = 0; offset < archive.byteLength; offset += partSize) {
 const part = archive.slice(offset, offset + partSize);
 const uploaded = await hive.databases.uploadPart(
 migration.id,
 partNumber,
 part,
 { idempotencyKey: `workspace-upload-part-${partNumber}` },
 );
 if (!uploaded.ok) throw new Error(uploaded.error);
 partNumber += 1;
}

await hive.databases.completeUpload(
 migration.id,
 { idempotencyKey: "workspace-upload-complete-2026-08-24" },
);
```

Upload from your server, never from a browser holding an API key.

```ts
const started = await hive.databases.beginDownload(
 { confirmation: HIVEMINDOS_DATABASE_CONFIRMATIONS.migrateToLocal },
 { idempotencyKey: "workspace-download-2026-08-24" },
);

if (!started.ok) throw new Error(started.error);

const response = await hive.databases.downloadArchive(started.migration.id);
if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Archive download failed: ${response.status}`);

const bytes = new Uint8Array(await response.arrayBuffer());
const expectedSha256 = response.headers.get("X-Content-Sha256");
```

- Wait until `getTransfer` reports `archiveReady: true` before downloading.
- Check the bytes against `X-Content-Sha256` before importing. `X-HivemindOS-Archive-Public-Key` shows where the archive came from.
- Transfers expire, so keep the archive in your own storage if you need it later. Cancel an unneeded copy with `cancelTransfer` and an idempotency key.

A connection protects the credential for an API, OAuth account, wallet, database, remote tool server, or custom integration.

```ts
const created = await hive.connections.create(
 {
 name: "Customer support source",
 kind: "api_key",
 serviceId: "hive-research",
 credentials: {
 apiKey: process.env.SUPPORT_SOURCE_KEY!,
 },
 metadata: {
 environment: "production",
 authMode: "header",
 credentialField: "apiKey",
 targetName: "x-customer-source-key",
 },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "support-source-production-v1" },
);
```

The response returns only `credentialFields`, such as `["apiKey"]`, never the values. Updating `credentials` rotates the stored value; deleting the connection removes it.

`metadata` holds labels and the credential mapping, never secrets:

- `authMode`: `bearer`, `header`, or `body`.
- `credentialField`: which protected field to use.
- `targetName`: required for header and body modes. Reserved HivemindOS headers cannot be replaced.

```ts
const result = await hive.apiKeys.create(
 {
 label: "Bounded worker",
 scopes: [
 "services:read",
 "services:invoke",
 "wallets:read",
 "wallets:transact",
 ],
 allowedServices: ["hive-research", "managed-wallets"],
 limits: {
 "*": {
 requestsPerHour: 2_000,
 maxConcurrent: 20,
 },
 "services.invoke": {
 requestsPerMinute: 120,
 },
 "services.invoke.hive-research": {
 requestsPerMinute: 30,
 maxConcurrent: 3,
 },
 "services.invoke.hive-research.analyses.create": {
 requestsPerDay: 250,
 },
 "services.invoke.cloud-superbrain.memory.answer": {
 requestsPerMinute: 10,
 requestsPerDay: 500,
 },
 "wallets.transactions.create": {
 requestsPerDay: 10,
 creditsPerDay: 25,
 },
 "credits.x402.topUp": {
 requestsPerDay: 4,
 },
 },
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "bounded-worker-v1" },
);
```

Add a remote HTTP MCP server, using your client's secret or environment-variable feature for the key:

```json
{
 "mcpServers": {
 "hivemindos": {
 "type": "http",
 "url": "https://api.hivemindos.app/mcp",
 "headers": {
 "Authorization": "Bearer ${HIVEMINDOS_API_KEY}"
 }
 }
 }
}
```

If your client has an **Add remote MCP server** screen, enter the address and add `Authorization` as a protected header. Never paste the key into a shared config file, prompt, repository, screenshot, or browser app.

An MCP key needs:

- the `services:read` scope;
- the `actions.list` operation, so the server can open the session and return its action catalog; and
- only the extra scopes, services, and exact operations the agent's job needs.

This key can search Cloud Superbrain memory and do nothing else:

```json
{
 "scopes": ["services:read", "services:invoke"],
 "allowedServices": ["cloud-superbrain"],
 "allowedOperations": [
 "actions.list",
 "services.invoke.cloud-superbrain.memory.search"
 ],
 "limits": {
 "services.invoke.cloud-superbrain.memory.search": {
 "requestsPerMinute": 30,
 "maxConcurrent": 3
 }
 }
}
```

Make keys in the [console](https://hivemindos.app/superagent-api/console/#keys) or through the API ([authentication guide](authentication.html)).

Lexical search is free and predictable, good for autocomplete and retrieval before a model call:

```ts
const search = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "cloud-superbrain",
 "memory.search",
 undefined,
 {
 query: {
 q: "customer-funded compute",
 project: "Aurora",
 memoryType: "decision",
 limit: 8,
 },
 idempotencyKey: "aurora-memory-search-001",
 },
);
```

Semantic recall finds memories even when the wording differs:

```ts
const recalled = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "cloud-superbrain",
 "memory.recall",
 {
 query: "Who pays before an Aurora GPU starts?",
 mode: "hybrid",
 project: "Aurora",
 limit: 8,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "aurora-memory-recall-001" },
);
```

Add `rerank: true` when the order matters, for example when an agent will read only the first few results. The best 20 matches are read again against the question and the most relevant are put first. It takes about a second longer and costs the same 1 credit, and the response says `reranked: true`. Only memories saved in the standard tier are read again; private and confidential ones keep their place. If the second read cannot run, you get the usual order and `reranked: false`. Rerank runs only in the standard tier: under the private or confidential base it is refused (409 `STANDARD_TIER_REQUIRED`), or, with `keywordFallback: true`, skipped with `rerankSkipped: "privacy-tier"`.

**Where the second read runs.** The second read is made by a reranking model at a third-party model provider (OpenRouter), not by HivemindOS. With `rerank: true`, the question and the text of up to 20 standard-tier memories (up to 12,000 characters of each) are sent to that provider, under its own terms. Memories saved in the private or confidential tier are never sent. Leave `rerank` off if your standard-tier memories must not leave HivemindOS.

On LongMemEval_S (500 questions, each answer among about 50 past conversations), recall puts the conversations that hold the answer in its top 5 97.7% of the time with `rerank: true`, and 93.7% without it. Both were 90.7% before September 28, 2026. [How it was measured](https://hivemindos.app/docs/for-users/features/shared-brain-benchmarks.html#hosted-memory-search-accuracy).

Memory Answer returns a finished, grounded response:

```ts
const answered = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "cloud-superbrain",
 "memory.answer",
 {
 query: "What is the Aurora compute funding policy?",
 limit: 8,
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "aurora-memory-answer-001" },
);

console.log(answered.result.answer);
console.log(answered.result.citedMemoryIds);
```

If no memory supports an answer, the request returns not found and releases the credit reservation, so you are not charged. For a question of four or more words, a memory counts as support when it matches at least two of the question's words, the exact phrase, or its meaning; one shared word is not enough. When the only candidates are weak meaning matches (see below), the answer model reads the best three; if its answer cites none of them, you get the same not-found reply and are not charged.

About results:

- Every hit includes its memory fields, an excerpt (the part holding the most question words), a total score, matched signals, and score details.
- A hit matched only as `semantic-weak` is close in meaning but not close enough to count as a strong match. Weak matches rank below every strong or worded match, so a short fact phrased differently from the question ("what pet do I have" against "I adopted a grey cat") is still found. Check the score before relying on one.
- Search matches word forms (`weddings` finds `wedding`), and rare words count for more than common ones.
- Operational memories such as action and handoff receipts are left out of default recall. Ask for a specific operational `memoryType` or set `includeOperational: true` when you want them.
- Set `trackUsage: false` for diagnostic lookups that should not affect usage ranking. `memory.usage` records retrieved or final-answer use for

Add `stream: true` to `memory.answer` to show the answer as it is written instead of after the whole of it. The response is server-sent events (`text/event-stream`) instead of the JSON envelope:

| Event | Data |
|---|---|
| `meta` | Everything the buffered answer returns except `answer`: hits, `citedMemoryIds`, `reader`, `privacy`, the conversation evidence, and `chargedCredits`. Sent first. |
| `delta` | `{ "text": "..." }`: the next part of the answer. |
| `done` | `{ "answer": "...", "chargedCredits": 5 }`: the whole answer, once it is complete. |
| `error` | `{ "error": "...", "chargedCredits": 5 }`: the reader stopped after it started. |

It costs the same as a buffered answer, charged once. The charge is settled when the reader's first words arrive, before the stream starts, and the response's `X-HivemindOS-Credits-Charged` header says how much. So a reader that fails before writing anything is not charged, and you get the usual JSON error instead of a stream. An answer you stop reading halfway, or one cut off by an `error` event, was still charged once and is not charged again. A request that finds nothing returns the same not-found JSON as without `stream`, uncharged. When the only candidates are weak meaning matches, the answer is read whole first (it is charged only if it cites them) and arrives as one `delta`. A stream is not replayed for a repeated idempotency key; `mode: "evidence"` has no answer to stream and refuses `stream: true`.

With the SDK:

```ts
import { collectMemoryAnswer } from "@hivemindos/sdk";

const started = await hive.memory.answerStream({ query: "When is my driving test?" }, { endUserId: "user-42" });
if (!started.ok) throw new Error(started.error);
for await (const event of started.events) {
 if (event.type === "delta") process.stdout.write(event.text);
}
// Or read it whole: const { answer, complete } = await collectMemoryAnswer(started);
```

Measured on 2026-10-06 through `/v1` with the SDK and the default reader (10 answers each, median): a paragraph-long answer's first words arrived in 1.03 s against 1.89 s for the buffered answer; a one-sentence answer in 0.99 s against 1.21 s; a "when" question, which the reader reasons about before it writes, in 2.15 s against 2.35 s. The longer the answer, the more streaming saves.

Memory Answer has a default reader, and you can name another. `memory.models` lists every model that can read an answer, with its privacy tier, reasoning settings, per-token price, and the most one answer can cost. The default is marked `default: true`. It needs no key.

```ts
const readers = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "cloud-superbrain",
 "memory.models",
 {},
 { idempotencyKey: "memory-readers-001" },
);
```

Pass a reader's `id` as `model`, and `reasoningEffort` (`none`, `low`, `medium` or `high`) when that reader lists it:

```ts
const answered = await hive.services.invokeOperation(
 "cloud-superbrain",
 "memory.answer",
 {
 query: "What is the Aurora compute funding policy?",
 model: "@cf/openai/gpt-oss-120b",
 reasoningEffort: "medium",
 },
 { idempotencyKey: "aurora-memory-answer-002" },
);

console.log(answered.result.reader);
```

A setting a reader does not take is refused before anything is charged, never quietly changed. The response's `reader` names the model that answered.

**Price.** With the default reader, an answer costs 5 credits. Another reader adds what it costs above the default for the same question, at HivemindOS rates, rounded up to a whole credit. A reader that costs less than the default still costs 5 credits. The answer first reserves the reader's `maximumCredits`, then charges for the question actually sent and releases the rest.

**Private and confidential answers.** Pass `private: true`, or call through the private base URL, to have the answer read only by a model that keeps nothing it is sent. Under the confidential base, name a reader that runs in an attested enclave as `model`; there is no default there. In both cases the answer never uses Turbo ordering, and `turbo: true` is refused. `memory.models` under a tier's base, or with `privacy` in its query, lists the readers that qualify.

A memory keeps the strictest tier it was written in and is read only by a reader that meets it. See [private and confidential access](privacy.html).

Measured reader accuracy and speed are on the [memory benchmarks](benchmarks.html) page.

When one of your app's users deletes their account, `memory.erase` removes everything they have in memory in one call: every memory, its past versions, its search indexes, and the brain's history. It is free, needs no approval, and is safe to repeat.

```ts
const erased = await hive.memory.erase({ endUserId: "user-42" });
// Same as hive.services.invokeOperation("cloud-superbrain", "memory.erase", {}, { endUserId: "user-42" })
if (!erased.ok) throw new Error(erased.error);
console.log(erased.result.memoriesErased, erased.result.complete);
```

- **Only one end user, only yours.** It erases the brain of the end user the call names, inside the key's own account and project, and nothing else: not another end user, not another project's brain for the same end user id, not the account's own memories. The end user comes from a key bound with `endUserId` (which erases only its own end user, and cannot name another) or from `X-HivemindOS-End-User` on an unbound key. A call that names no end user is refused with 400 `end_user_required`; an account's or a project's whole brain cannot be erased this way.
- **Which keys can call it.** Any key with the `services:invoke` scope whose operations include `services.invoke.cloud-superbrain.memory.erase` (a key with no operation list has every operation). To give an app server only this, allow just that selector. A key bound to an end user can erase only that end user, so a key you hand to a user's device can erase that user and no one else.
- **Repeating is safe.** A second erase finds nothing and returns `memoriesErased: 0`. A very large brain (over 5,000 memories) returns `complete: false`; call again until `complete` is true.
- **Audited.** Each erase leaves a record of when it ran, for which account and project, and how many memories went. It holds no memory content and not the end user's id. The call also appears in your [usage and audit events](usage-audit.html).

When new information replaces an active memory, use `memory.evolve`. The old record becomes `superseded`, the new one becomes the active head, and both stay linked.

